Evidence rules:
- Follow every schema and field description literally. Use the page image to
  align labels and value columns, and use the prepared page text above to
  transcribe exact text.
- For every value, transcribe the source first and then apply only the
  transformations required by that field's description. Preserve every source
  character or delimiter that the description does not explicitly remove or
  reformat. Never borrow a transformation from another field.
- Build rows before assigning headings. Enumerate every visual row with a value
  in at least one data column named by the selected array schema, and emit that
  row exactly once. A bold, indented, or subgroup label with data is still the
  subject of a data row.
- Reconcile that enumeration against the supplied page-table row inventory.
  Every inventoried source line with a value in a schema data column must map to
  one emitted row. Ignore inventoried headers, section-only rows, separators,
  and blanks rather than treating every inventoried line as data.
- For a field defined as the nearest preceding section-only or group heading, a
  line qualifies only when its visual row has no values in any data column.
  Assign headings only after all data rows have been enumerated. A data-bearing
  label must retain the preceding qualifying heading and must not replace it.
- Apply the active qualifying heading to every following data row until another
  qualifying heading appears. When a field description names a heading category,
  use only the printed line or block that satisfies that category. Do not append
  an adjacent title, subtitle, description, or document label.
- Before returning, audit every string-valued cell against its field
  description. Removing whitespace inside a source delimiter never removes the
  delimiter itself. Preserve all source delimiters unless that field description
  explicitly says to remove them. Whitespace-only normalization inside a
  delimiter pair changes `( value )` to `(value)`, never to `value`.
- Before returning, audit every carried heading field. If its value is copied
  from a row that has any data-column value, it is not a qualifying heading;
  restore the nearest preceding value-less heading, or emit null on a
  continuation page so the ordered parser can carry it.
- For a category-qualified heading, stop before an adjacent line whose role is
  a title, subtitle, content-type label, or description rather than part of that
  category heading.

Continuation rules:
- Audit the table continuation evidence against any current-of-total marker in
  the page text. For a marker belonging to the table, current 1 means a new
  table page and current greater than 1 means a continuation. Use the marker
  when a supplied continuation flag contradicts it.
- A table marked as a continuation after that audit is not a new schema unit,
  whether or not headers repeat.
- For a new table or source block, set unit_name to the exact array unit name
  from the schema units supplied above and set is_continuation to false.
- For a continuation, set is_continuation to true. If the owning unit is not
  explicitly identifiable on this page, set unit_name to null; the ordered
  parser will attach it to the preceding record. Do not classify continuation
  rows by their subject matter.
- Leave an aggregation-policy carry field null when its value is not printed on
  this continuation page. The ordered parser carries the preceding value.
- For aggregation-policy sequence fields, emit a page-local 1-based ordinal.
  The ordered parser assigns the document-wide sequence.
- rows contains only values visible on this page and must match the selected
  unit's item schema. Do not emit rows from another page.
- source.page_index is the current zero-based page index. source.table_index is
  the zero-based index in the supplied page-tables array, or null for a
  non-table block.
- Never invent a continuation, unit name, or value.

